  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Npc191948 and HPAC through a series of target interactions involving the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14. This subunit is identified as a target of multiple compounds, including 5alpha,6alpha-Epoxy(22e,24r)-ergosta-8(14),22-dien-3beta,7alpha-diol, (6aR,9R)-N-[(1S,2S,4R,7S)-2-hydroxy-5-oxo-4,7-di(propan-2-yl)-3-oxa-6,9-diazatricyclo[7.3.0.02,6]dodecan-4-yl]-7-methyl-6,6a,8,9-tetrahydro-4H-indolo[4,3-fg]quinoline-9-carboxamide, and (+)-(5S,7R)-7-Ethoxy-12-Hydroxy-2-Oxo-20-Norabieta-1(10),8,11,13-Tetraene. These compounds all share a common pathway of interaction with the 26S proteasome subunit, which in turn is linked to HPAC. The scores from the paths indicate a consistent pattern of interaction, suggesting that Npc191948 may be involved in the regulation of HPAC through this subunit. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Npc191948 and HPAC, primarily through the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, but further experimental validation would be necessary to confirm the direct relationship.",
